1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:07,675
This is Nativeproof, a research workspace that tests how a campaign may land in another culture before money is spent on it.

2
00:00:07,675 --> 00:00:18,912
Everything in this video is one real session on the deployed app, recorded on October twenty-ninth: live Qloo cultural data, live language models, and synthetic participants.

3
00:00:18,912 --> 00:00:21,487
The participants are generated by AI.

4
00:00:21,487 --> 00:00:25,650
They are not real people, and the app labels them that way everywhere.

5
00:00:29,454 --> 00:00:31,541
We sign in with the judging account.

6
00:00:31,541 --> 00:00:39,191
Google sign-in is on the same page, and Try the demo starts an anonymous session on clearly labeled fixture data.

7
00:00:39,191 --> 00:00:41,841
This session uses live mode only.

8
00:00:45,658 --> 00:00:53,220
We create a live project for a fictional brand, Trailhead Coffee, launching canned cold brew for weekend hikers in Japan.

9
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:58,945
The brand kit records the voice, terms that must stay verbatim, and terms that are not allowed.

10
00:00:58,945 --> 00:01:03,733
Then we add the campaign copy and the cultural references to investigate.

11
00:01:03,733 --> 00:01:10,333
Every edit creates a new content version, so each later result names exactly which copy it tested.

12
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:17,612
Atlas resolves the campaign's concepts against Qloo.

13
00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:21,262
In this live run, all five terms came back ambiguous.

14
00:01:21,262 --> 00:01:28,312
Qloo holds several tags named Hiking, for example as a hobby, an activity and a lifestyle.

15
00:01:28,312 --> 00:01:30,337
Nativeproof does not guess.

16
00:01:30,337 --> 00:01:34,137
It lists the candidates, and we confirm one with Use this.

17
00:01:34,137 --> 00:01:40,250
Each choice re-runs discovery, and each run took between thirty and sixty-six seconds.

18
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:43,437
With all five confirmed, ten signals resolved.

19
00:01:43,437 --> 00:01:51,262
The market brief separates source data from model interpretation, and its risks state plainly that affinity data does not prove demand.

20
00:01:55,074 --> 00:02:02,236
A population study builds on a study, so we create one with two research questions and run the quick rehearsal.

21
00:02:02,236 --> 00:02:09,486
It generated twelve synthetic reactions grounded in ten discovery evidence records, in fifty-six seconds.

22
00:02:09,486 --> 00:02:16,799
It raised something we had not considered: several reactions were unsure how hikers would carry and dispose of the can.

23
00:02:20,614 --> 00:02:21,951
Now the main study.

24
00:02:21,951 --> 00:02:35,276
We build a new synthetic population of one hundred participants for Japan at standard interview depth, and we upload the ad creative, so participants react to what a vision model actually observes in the image.

25
00:02:35,276 --> 00:02:38,526
The study runs in the background and saves checkpoints.

26
00:02:38,526 --> 00:02:45,026
It can be cancelled or resumed, and a server restart marks it resumable instead of losing the work.

27
00:02:48,842 --> 00:02:54,117
The study finished in two minutes and twenty seconds, and all one hundred interviews completed.

28
00:02:54,117 --> 00:03:01,204
Seventy-one percent were positive, twelve mixed and seventeen neutral, with none negative or confused.

29
00:03:01,204 --> 00:03:06,229
The participants come from four groups built from the Qloo evidence, twenty-five in each.

30
00:03:06,229 --> 00:03:10,517
Every number is a tally of stored interviews, not a model's estimate.

31
00:03:10,517 --> 00:03:16,304
The reaction graph links campaign elements, groups, reactions and themes.

32
00:03:16,304 --> 00:03:19,554
The landscape maps the groups to their Qloo interests.

33
00:03:19,554 --> 00:03:28,792
Every interview is labeled as AI-generated, and the recommendations name their own gaps, such as the absence of negative voices.

34
00:03:32,598 --> 00:03:35,523
Next, a three round social simulation.

35
00:03:35,523 --> 00:03:44,010
In each round, twenty percent of the participants read a feed of five peer posts, then post, comment, share or ignore.

36
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:50,498
It completed in seventy seconds, with nine, four and eight stance changes across the rounds.

37
00:03:50,498 --> 00:04:01,260
Each post records the stance before and after, the participant's stated reason, and the exact posts it reacted to, so every claimed influence can be traced back to its source.

38
00:04:05,074 --> 00:04:06,636
Participants keep memory.

39
00:04:06,636 --> 00:04:12,799
This participant's current stance comes from round one, and its memories are its interview and its own post.

40
00:04:12,799 --> 00:04:16,411
We ask a follow-up about carrying the empty can back down.

41
00:04:16,411 --> 00:04:20,536
The answer cites one stored memory and is saved as a new memory.

42
00:04:20,536 --> 00:04:22,399
We can also question a whole group.

43
00:04:22,399 --> 00:04:27,461
A seeded sample of five answered, and the tally covers only that sample.

44
00:04:31,262 --> 00:04:34,812
Risk Radar scans the copy and the image together.

45
00:04:34,812 --> 00:04:39,662
A vision model, MiniCPM-V, analyzed the uploaded creative.

46
00:04:39,662 --> 00:04:45,474
In twenty-seven seconds the scan found five potential issues, one moderate and four low.

47
00:04:45,474 --> 00:04:54,587
The moderate one is the line, crack one open at the summit, which the model reads as a possible clash with etiquette at sacred peaks in Japan.

48
00:04:54,587 --> 00:04:57,974
That reading is labeled as model interpretation.

49
00:04:57,974 --> 00:05:01,837
Applying the suggested change creates a new adaptation.

50
00:05:01,837 --> 00:05:05,224
The campaign itself is never edited in place.

51
00:05:09,042 --> 00:05:14,967
Voice writes market adaptations, grounded in the discovery evidence and the rehearsal findings.

52
00:05:14,967 --> 00:05:18,517
It produced two variants in about fifteen seconds.

53
00:05:18,517 --> 00:05:29,554
Each change shows what it replaces, why, and whether evidence supports it, and deterministic checks confirm that protected terms were kept and prohibited terms avoided.

54
00:05:33,358 --> 00:05:38,370
Loop scores the original and three candidates under one scoring version.

55
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:43,283
The best candidate scored ninety point five against eighty-five point eight.

56
00:05:43,283 --> 00:05:50,983
Because the margin is under five points, the decision rule recommends validating with real people instead of declaring a winner.

57
00:05:54,794 --> 00:05:58,344
Loop can also compare versions on identical participants.

58
00:05:58,344 --> 00:06:04,294
We saved the risk fix as version three and interviewed the same one hundred participants again.

59
00:06:04,294 --> 00:06:08,706
Reusing the population skipped all Qloo and construction work.

60
00:06:08,706 --> 00:06:14,856
The result is honest: positive reactions fell from seventy-one to sixty-six percent.

61
00:06:14,856 --> 00:06:22,369
The fix may remove a cultural risk but cost emotional pull, and the app states that no statistical significance is claimed.

62
00:06:26,190 --> 00:06:32,077
Campaign Arena puts concepts side by side for the same synthetic audience and the same criteria.

63
00:06:32,077 --> 00:06:41,252
In eighty seconds, variant B led with ninety-three point eight out of a hundred, and the original was marked as within five points of the leader.

64
00:06:49,118 --> 00:06:54,030
Market Expansion runs the same discovery across several markets.

65
00:06:54,030 --> 00:06:56,355
Here it shows a real limitation.

66
00:06:56,355 --> 00:07:09,055
Its terms came back ambiguous in both Japan and South Korea, and this screen does not yet have a confirmation step, so both markets are reported as unavailable instead of being ranked on missing data.

67
00:07:12,858 --> 00:07:16,958
Brand Memory stores the rules every workflow reads.

68
00:07:16,958 --> 00:07:22,095
Trend Watch compares two saved discovery snapshots without calling a model.

69
00:07:22,095 --> 00:07:31,608
It shows which signals appeared and disappeared, and it warns that two snapshots are not a trend line, and that a difference can come from matching changes rather than culture.

70
00:07:35,422 --> 00:07:43,972
The Evidence Graph draws only links the system stored: one hundred and fifty-three nodes and one hundred and seventy edges from this session.

71
00:07:43,972 --> 00:07:46,847
Selecting a node shows its provenance.

72
00:07:46,847 --> 00:07:53,634
This one is a cached Qloo source signal, produced by a discovery run, with its limitations attached.

73
00:07:57,434 --> 00:08:02,659
Finally, Proof assembles a report in thirteen seconds without calling a model.

74
00:08:02,659 --> 00:08:14,059
It carries the verdict, the evidence register, the limitations, and a reproducibility section with model identifiers, prompt versions, and each stage's provider and latency.

75
00:08:14,059 --> 00:08:17,959
It exports as Markdown, JSON or PDF.

76
00:08:19,274 --> 00:08:27,586
Nativeproof helps teams find cultural risks and opportunities before launch, and it says clearly when the evidence is not enough.

77
00:08:27,586 --> 00:08:31,836
Synthetic participants are a tool for forming hypotheses.

78
00:08:31,836 --> 00:08:35,224
Decisions still need validation with real people.

79
00:08:35,224 --> 00:08:38,699
The code, diagrams and setup guide are on GitHub.
